---
import Post from "../../layouts/Post.astro";
import { SITE, DEFAULT_OG, iso, POSTS, authorNode, publisherNode, websiteNode, topicsForTags } from "../../lib/blog.mjs";

const slug = "hire-web-developer-india-from-us";
const idx = POSTS.findIndex((p) => p.slug === slug);
const post = POSTS[idx];

const canonical = `${SITE}/blog/${slug}/`;
const ogImage = post.coverImage ? `${SITE}${post.coverImage}` : DEFAULT_OG;
const primaryTag = post.tags[0] || "Notes";
const readMins = post.readMins;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is it safe to hire a web developer in India?",
    a: "As safe as hiring anywhere, provided you're clear which market you're buying from. India has large low-cost body shops and it has senior independents running small practices, and almost every horror story comes from someone buying the first while expecting the second. Vet the individual, not the country.",
  },
  {
    q: "How much does it cost to hire a web developer in India?",
    a: "Less than the equivalent seniority in the US or UK, but the honest range is wide enough that a single figure would mislead you. Rates track experience rather than geography more than people expect \u2014 a senior independent in India is not priced like an offshore team, and shouldn't be. Insist on a fixed written quote after a scoping call.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do time zones work with a developer in India?",
    a: "IST overlaps US mornings, UK afternoons and Gulf evenings, which is enough for a real-time call most days without anyone working unsociable hours. The bigger factor is working style: async written updates and a staging link you can check any time matter more than overlapping hours.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I pay an Indian developer from the US or UK?",
    a: "International bank transfer or a platform like Wise is standard, usually against milestone invoices. Expect a proper invoice with the right details for your accounting. Milestone billing protects both sides \u2014 you're never far ahead on payment, and neither is the work.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I avoid the offshore horror stories?",
    a: "Almost all of them share a shape: no written scope, a price that was too good, no live work checked, and communication that was already vague before money changed hands. Check something they built on your own phone, get the scope in writing, pay in milestones, and treat slow or unclear replies during the sales conversation as information.",
  },
];

const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BlogPosting",
      "@id": `${canonical}#post`,
      headline: post.title,
      description: post.description,
      datePublished: iso(post.pubDate),
      dateModified: iso(post.updatedDate || post.pubDate),
      author: authorNode(SITE),
      publisher: publisherNode(SITE),
      isPartOf: websiteNode(SITE),
      mainEntityOfPage: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
      image: ogImage,
      articleSection: primaryTag,
      keywords: post.tags.join(", "),
      // Resolves the free-text tags above to public concept identifiers, so a
      // parser knows "Astro" means the framework and "GEO" isn't geography.
      about: topicsForTags(post.tags),
      wordCount: post.wordCount,
      timeRequired: `PT${readMins}M`,
      speakable: {
        "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
        cssSelector: [".post-title", ".post-lead"],
      },
      inLanguage: "en",
      url: canonical,
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Blog", item: `${SITE}/blog/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: post.title, item: canonical },
      ],
    },
    {
      // FAQ rich results were retired by Google in May 2026, so this earns no
      // SERP feature. It stays because answer engines still parse it and every
      // question below is rendered visibly on the page.
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ "@type": "Question", name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a } })),
    },
  ],
});
---

<Post post={post} jsonld={jsonld} faqs={faqs}>
<p>If you’re a founder in the US, UK or Europe, you’ve heard both versions of the India story.</p>
<p>Version one: incredible engineering value, half the cost, great English, overnight progress while you sleep. Version two: a bargain website that arrived broken, three months late, built by someone you never actually spoke to.</p>
<p>Both versions are true. They describe different markets that happen to share a country. This guide is about telling them apart — written by an Indian developer who works with international clients and has no incentive to pretend the second market doesn’t exist.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="the-two-markets">The two markets</h2>
<p><strong>Market one: the body shops.</strong> Agencies selling hours in bulk. The person who sold you never writes code; the person writing code never talked to you. Your project is a ticket queue. The price looks unbeatable because the unit being sold is time, not outcomes — and rework time bills too.</p>
<p><strong>Market two: senior independents.</strong> A newer generation — developers who could work at product companies but chose independence, who design and code themselves, ship for global clients, and communicate like the remote-native professionals they are. You work with the actual maker, in writing, against outcomes.</p>
<p>The tragedy of “hiring in India” horror stories is that buyers price-shopped their way into market one while believing they were hiring market two.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="what-fair-pricing-actually-looks-like">How to tell which market a quote came from</h2>
<p>Forget the number for a moment and read the quote itself. The tell is never the price — it’s what the price is attached to.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Suspiciously cheap, vaguely scoped:</strong> market one. Something in that scope is fictional — usually the “custom” part, sometimes the deadline. Ask what “custom” means and you’ll get a theme name.</li>
<li><strong>Quoted after a conversation, fixed, in writing, with the deliverables named:</strong> market two. A senior independent can tell you what ships, what waits, and what they’d cut if you needed it cheaper — because they’re the one building it.</li>
<li><strong>Priced by the hour with no ceiling:</strong> you’re buying time, not an outcome, and rework time bills too.</li>
</ul>
<p>You’re not saving 90% by hiring in India. You’re saving the 50–70% that was agency overhead — while the engineering quality, at the senior end, is identical. Chasing the missing 90% is how you end up in market one.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="the-vetting-checklist">The vetting checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Talk to the person who’ll write the code.</strong> If you can’t, walk. This single filter removes most of market one.</li>
<li><strong>Live production URLs.</strong> Open their work on your phone. Fast? Polished? Still online? Portfolios lie; production doesn’t.</li>
<li><strong>A written scope within 48 hours.</strong> What ships, what it costs, what’s explicitly out. Seniors volunteer this.</li>
<li><strong>Code ownership in writing.</strong> Full repository, IP transfer on final payment. Standard for market two, mysteriously complicated in market one.</li>
<li><strong>Async fluency.</strong> Clear written English, Loom walkthroughs, staging links without being asked. The IST timezone (UTC+5:30) means your morning overlaps their evening — enough for a daily sync, and progress lands while you sleep.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2 id="how-the-engagement-actually-runs">How the engagement actually runs</h2>
<p>With a senior independent, a typical build looks like: a scoping call, a fixed written quote, milestone payments, a staging URL updated continuously, and handover with documentation. My fastest full build — a <a href="/projects/modernkbs/">site plus admissions portal</a> — took seven days; typical is three to four weeks. That cadence is normal for market two, not exceptional.</p>
<p>One more thing worth demanding from any offshore build: portability. The site should run anywhere — no dependence on the developer’s hosting, no platform you can’t leave. I’ve written up why that matters in <a href="/blog/own-your-stack-code-portability/">own your stack</a>.</p>
<hr>
<p><em>I’m Divyansh Sood, an independent developer in Himachal Pradesh, India, building custom-coded sites, stores and <a href="/services/web-app-development/">web apps</a> for founders in the US, UK and Europe — fixed quotes in writing, full code ownership. If you want to calibrate against a real one before you hire anyone, <a href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com">email me your brief</a> — I’ll respond within a day with an honest scope, even if the honest answer is that you don’t need me.</em></p>
</Post>
